Your app can get a trigger’s payload either by **subscribing locally with the SDK** or by **registering a webhook for production**.

*For quick prototyping* use the SDK’s `subscribe()` method, which opens a Pusher‑backed WebSocket and streams events to a callback in your process.  
```typescript
await composio.triggers.subscribe(
  data => console.log('Event:', data),
  { triggerId: 'your_trigger_id' }
);
```  
See the “Quick look with `subscribe()`” section [/docs/setting-up-triggers/subscribing-to-events#quick-look-with-subscribe](/docs/setting-up-triggers/subscribing-to-events#quick-look-with-subscribe).

*For production* set a webhook URL so Composio POSTs each event to your server. The SDK can create the subscription and give you the secret for signature verification.  
```typescript
const sub = await composio.triggers.setWebhookSubscription({
  webhookUrl: 'https://your‑app.com/webhooks/composio',
});
console.log('Secret:', sub.secret);
```  
See the “Receive events in production” guide [/docs/setting-up-triggers/subscribing-to-events#receive-events-in-production](/docs/setting-up-triggers/subscribing-to-events#receive-events-in-production).

Both approaches are described in the **subscribing to events** guide [/docs/setting-up-triggers/subscribing-to-events](/docs/setting-up-triggers/subscribing-to-events).

